<section class="section" id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="kicker"><span class="kicker__dot"></span>What you get</p>
      <h2 class="display">A compact companion, not another dashboard to manage.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Usefulness follows from cadence: live numbers you can glance at, optional
        nudges when the rhythm dips, and a short practice so the habit is easier
        to feel — not only to measure.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="bento">
      <article class="card tile sage">
        <h3>Live cadence, one glance</h3>
        <p>
          Rate, regularity, and ORI on a compact dashboard, plus screen time
          (how long a face has actually been in front of the camera today) and a
          daily average.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card tile peach">
        <h3>Alert, if you ask</h3>
        <p>
          Optional sound if ORI stays low for a meaningful stretch — not a
          single bad second, but a sustained dip — so you can blink, look away,
          or rest before strain piles up.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card tile sun">
        <h3>Beep on each blink</h3>
        <p>
          A tighter feedback loop. Optional click on every counted blink, for
          days when you want the rhythm in your ear.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card tile mint">
        <h3>Practice</h3>
        <p>
          A short, full-screen coach: a countdown, then a guided two-minute
          session that paces blinks so the habit is easier to feel.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card tile sky">
        <h3>Built for the desk</h3>
        <p>
          A small window, optionally always on top, with a launch check-in about
          how comfortable the eyes feel. Camera preview stays put-away unless
          you open it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card tile lavender">
        <h3>This work, not the whole room</h3>
        <p>
          Blinks are counted when you are actually at the screen. Looking
          clearly away is treated as off-screen, so the score is about this
          stretch of work.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

